A 2012 Synology DS-212j is still a perfectly usable little box: a Marvell Kirkwood 88F6281 at 1.2 GHz, 256 MB of DDR2, two SATA bays, USB 2.0 and gigabit Ethernet. Synology stopped updating DSM for it long ago, and DSM 6.2 runs a 2.6.32 kernel. This write-up replaces DSM with Debian running a current upstream Linux kernel, and eventually swaps the 2012 vendor U-Boot for a modern mainline U-Boot.

<blockquote>Repository: https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j

'''Status:''' done. Mainline U-Boot v2026.07 is in SPI flash, and Debian 13 with Linux 7.2.10 boots from a RAID1 mirror on the two internal disks. Every step below was actually carried out on the hardware.
</blockquote>
''Patches, scripts and configuration files: [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j].''

<span id="credits-and-prior-work"></span>
== Credits and prior work ==

This project stands on two earlier write-ups:

* '''tangrs, [https://blog.tangrs.id.au/2021/11/19/running-debian-on-a-synology-ds212j/ &quot;Running Debian on a Synology DS212J&quot;] (19 November 2021)'''. It explains how DSM boots, why a Debian root under DSM's 2.6.32 kernel fails (<code>FATAL: kernel too old</code>), and how to boot Debian's <code>linux-image-marvell</code> from the stock U-Boot over TFTP with an appended DTB. Above all, it found the two most important board quirks: '''GPIO 29/31 power the drives''', and '''GPIO 35 is the fan tachometer, not an alarm''', so the upstream DT makes <code>systemd-udevd</code> spin. The DT changes from that post are in [https://gist.github.com/tangrs/c3e6bd13b849d5a5bcf9d338550427fe this gist]. Synology's GPL source, which both of us used, is on [https://sourceforge.net/projects/dsgpl/files/Synology%20NAS%20GPL%20Source/ SourceForge] and on Synology's own download site.
* '''The Debian wiki, [https://wiki.debian.org/InstallingDebianOn/Synology InstallingDebianOn/Synology]'''. It covers the DS218j (Armada 385, a different SoC), but its general notes apply here: the 3.3 V serial header (pins 2 = GND, 4 = TX, 6 = RX on the board; checked on the DS218j, not re-checked on this board), Ctrl-C to stop U-Boot, the appended-DTB <code>uImage</code> trick for old vendor U-Boots, and <code>make bindeb-pkg</code> for the kernel.

Where this guide goes further: a mainline U-Boot in flash (so no TFTP or serial is needed to boot), a fixed upstream DT (RAM size, SATA pinctrl, LEDs), the <code>sata_mv</code> LED patch, RAID1 root without an initramfs, Debian kernel packages, and the DSM LED/beep behaviour. The [[#appendix--the-device-tree-changes-explained|appendix]] says, for each DT change, where the information came from. A [[#coverage-compared-with-the-earlier-write-ups|coverage table]] at the end compares this guide with both sources.

<span id="what-you-need"></span>
== What you need ==

* The DS-212j. This guide wipes the disks, so back up anything you care about first.
* A 3.3 V USB-to-serial adapter connected to the board's console header (115200 8N1). Here it shows up as <code>/dev/ttyUSB0</code>.
* A Linux PC (Ubuntu/Debian-based here).
* One or two USB sticks.
* No network is required. Everything in this guide goes over the serial line and USB.

