<span id="step-1--get-to-the-u-boot-prompt"></span>
== Step 1 – Get to the U-Boot prompt ==

Connect the serial adapter, open the console at 115200 baud, power the NAS on, and press any key when you see <code>Hit any key to stop autoboot</code>. You land at the <code>Marvell&gt;&gt;</code> prompt.

To catch the prompt reliably from a script, I used [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/tools/catch_uboot.py <code>tools/catch_uboot.py</code>], which logs the console and spams Ctrl-C once the countdown starts.

<span id="step-2--back-up-the-whole-spi-flash-over-serial-only"></span>
== Step 2 – Back up the whole SPI flash (over serial only) ==

The stock U-Boot can't write to USB, but the flash is memory-mapped at <code>0xF8000000</code>, so we can simply <code>md</code> it and parse the hex dump on the PC. To be sure no byte is corrupted on the serial line, every 64 KB chunk is checked against U-Boot's own <code>crc32</code> command and re-read if it doesn't match:

<pre>python3 tools/dump_flash.py backup/flash_stock.bin</pre>
This takes about 25 minutes at 115200 baud (no chunk needed a retry here). Keep <code>backup/flash_stock.bin</code> safe: it is your way back to DSM, and the <code>vendor</code> area at <code>0x3C0000</code> holds the board's real MAC address (<code>00:11:32:…</code>) and serial number. The DSM kernel stored at <code>0x80000</code> turns out to be Linux 2.6.32.12.

<span id="extracting-the-ddr2-settings-from-the-stock-bootloader"></span>
=== Extracting the DDR2 settings from the stock bootloader ===

The first 512 bytes of the flash are the Kirkwood boot ROM header (<code>0x5a</code> = SPI boot, load to <code>0x600000</code>, entry point <code>0x670000</code>). Its extension header contains the register writes the boot ROM performs to initialise the DDR2 controller '''before''' U-Boot runs. A new U-Boot needs exactly these values, and they are saved in [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/backup/ddr_regs.txt <code>backup/ddr_regs.txt</code>]:

<pre>DATA 0xFFD01400 0x43000C30   # SDRAM config
DATA 0xFFD01404 0x39743000   # DDR controller control (low)
...
DATA 0xFFD01504 0x07FFFFF1   # CS0: 128 MB, enabled
DATA 0xFFD0150C 0x07FFFFF5   # CS1: 128 MB, enabled
...</pre>
<span id="pin-multiplexing"></span>
=== Pin multiplexing ===

The stock MPP (pin function) and GPIO registers can be read directly from the stock U-Boot prompt. They are saved in [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/backup/stock-mpp-gpio-regs.txt <code>backup/stock-mpp-gpio-regs.txt</code>]:

<pre>Marvell&gt;&gt; md.l f1010000 7
f1010000: 01002222 03303311 00550000 00000000
f1010010: 44440000 00044444 00000000</pre>
Decoded: MPP0-3 are SPI, MPP6 is SYSRST_OUTn, MPP8/9 are I²C (for the S-35390A RTC), MPP10/11 are UART0 (the console), and MPP13/14 are UART1 (the power/LED microcontroller). Everything else is GPIO. The audio functions on MPP36-44 are leftovers from Marvell's reference design; the DS-212j has no audio.

<span id="step-3--build-tools-host"></span>
== Step 3 – Build tools (host) ==

<pre>sudo apt install bison flex libssl-dev libgnutls28-dev uuid-dev bc gcc-arm-linux-gnueabi \
    mmdebstrap qemu-user-static binfmt-support arch-test debian-archive-keyring</pre>
Ubuntu's <code>gcc-arm-linux-gnueabi</code> targets <code>armv5t</code> with soft-float, which is exactly what the Kirkwood (ARMv5TE) and Debian's <code>armel</code> port need.

<span id="step-4--a-mainline-u-boot-for-the-ds-212j"></span>
== Step 4 – A mainline U-Boot for the DS-212j ==

Mainline U-Boot (v2026.07) still supports Kirkwood and has a Synology DS109 board, but nothing for the DS-212j. The port in [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/patches/u-boot/0001-arm-kirkwood-add-Synology-DS-212j-board.patch <code>patches/u-boot/</code>] adds a <code>ds212j</code> target:

* <code>kwbimage.cfg</code> contains the DDR2 register list copied '''verbatim''' from the stock bootloader header (step 2). This is the only part that can't be guessed.
* The pin setup matches the stock registers, and the HDD LEDs and fan GPIOs are initialised.
* It uses the upstream <code>kirkwood-ds212j</code> device tree, which U-Boot shares with Linux.
* It enables USB storage, SATA (<code>sata_mv</code>) for both bays, ext4/FAT, and '''bootstd''', so it finds <code>/boot/extlinux/extlinux.conf</code> on USB or SATA by itself.
* The environment is stored in SPI flash at <code>0x3D0000</code>, which was unused on this unit, and the <code>vendor</code> sector is left alone.

<pre>git clone --depth 1 -b v2026.07 https://source.denx.de/u-boot/u-boot.git
cd u-boot &amp;&amp; git am ../patches/u-boot/*.patch
make CROSS_COMPILE=arm-linux-gnueabi- O=../uboot-out ds212j_defconfig
make CROSS_COMPILE=arm-linux-gnueabi- O=../uboot-out -j8
# -&gt; ../uboot-out/u-boot.kwb (≈ 320 KiB, fits in the 512 KiB bootloader slot)</pre>
(On Ubuntu, the host <code>kwboot</code> tool links against <code>-ltinfo</code>. Install <code>libncurses-dev</code>, or point <code>HOSTLDFLAGS=-L…</code> to a directory with a <code>libtinfo.so</code> symlink.)

<span id="step-5--linux-72-for-the-ds-212j"></span>
== Step 5 – Linux 7.2 for the DS-212j ==

The upstream <code>kirkwood-ds212j.dts</code> has several bugs: wrong RAM size, a SATA pin conflict, drives left unpowered, dark disk LEDs, and a fan interrupt storm. The [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/tree/main/patches/linux DT patch] fixes them. Each change, and how I found it, is explained in the [[#appendix--the-device-tree-changes-explained|appendix]]. The kernel is built from <code>mvebu_v5_defconfig</code> plus a [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/config/linux-ds212j.config config fragment] that adds what systemd/Debian need (cgroups, seccomp, autofs, IPv6…), MD RAID1, the board drivers (gpio-fan, LEDs, S-35390A RTC, Synology power-off via UART1) and appended-DTB support so the stock U-Boot can boot it too.

<pre>tar xf linux-7.2.10.tar.xz &amp;&amp; cd linux-7.2.10
for p in ../patches/linux/*.patch; do patch -p1 &lt; $p; done
export ARCH=arm CROSS_COMPILE=arm-linux-gnueabi-
make O=../linux-out mvebu_v5_defconfig
scripts/kconfig/merge_config.sh -m -O ../linux-out ../linux-out/.config ../config/linux-ds212j.config
make O=../linux-out olddefconfig
make O=../linux-out -j8 zImage modules dtbs        # ~7 min on an 8-thread laptop</pre>
<span id="step-6--a-debian-13-trixie-armel-root-filesystem-built-without-root"></span>
== Step 6 – A Debian 13 (trixie) armel root filesystem, built without root ==

Trixie is the last Debian release with <code>armel</code>. Its userland is fine, but it no longer ships a Kirkwood kernel, which is why we built one. <code>mmdebstrap</code> in <code>unshare</code> mode builds the rootfs as a normal user, running the armel binaries through qemu-user:

<pre>mmdebstrap --mode=unshare --arch=armel --variant=minbase \
  --include=systemd-sysv,udev,openssh-server,sudo,mdadm,u-boot-tools,… \
  trixie debian-trixie-armel.tar http://deb.debian.org/debian</pre>
[https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/tools/make-usb-image.sh <code>tools/make-usb-image.sh</code>] then turns it into a bootable disk image, still without root. Inside a user namespace (<code>unshare --map-auto --map-root-user</code>), it extracts the tarball with correct ownership, installs the kernel, DTB and modules, writes <code>extlinux.conf</code>, <code>fstab</code>, the hostname and DHCP config, sets a root password, and runs <code>mkfs.ext4 -d</code> on the result. A tiny Python snippet writes the MBR, so <code>sfdisk</code> isn't needed either.

<pre>tools/make-usb-image.sh debian-trixie-armel.tar linux-out ds212j-usb.img 2500M
sudo dd if=ds212j-usb.img of=/dev/sdX bs=4M conv=fsync status=progress</pre>
<span id="step-7--test-the-new-u-boot-from-ram-with-kwboot-nothing-is-flashed"></span>
== Step 7 – Test the new U-Boot from RAM with <code>kwboot</code> (nothing is flashed) ==

The Kirkwood boot ROM can load a bootloader over the UART. <code>kwboot</code> (built along with U-Boot) keeps sending the boot pattern while the board resets, and the ROM then takes the image over XMODEM instead of reading SPI flash. There's no need to touch the power button. Type <code>reset</code> at the stock prompt (or <code>reboot</code> in Linux), and start kwboot right away:

<pre>uboot-out/tools/kwboot -b uboot-out/u-boot.kwb -B 115200 /dev/ttyUSB0</pre>
[https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/tools/kwboot_test.py <code>tools/kwboot_test.py</code>] automates the reset, the upload and the console log. The upload takes about 35 s. On the first try:

<pre>U-Boot 2026.07 (Oct 13 2026 - 14:32:39 +0200)
Synology DS-212j
SoC:   Kirkwood 88F6281_A1
Model: Synology DS212j v10, v20
DRAM:  256 MiB
...
** Booting bootflow 'usb_mass_storage.lun0.bootdev.part_1' with extlinux
Starting kernel ...
Linux version 7.2.10-ds212j ...
Welcome to Debian GNU/Linux 13 (trixie)!
ds212j login:</pre>
<span id="step-8--three-bugs-on-the-way-to-working-disks"></span>
== Step 8 – Three bugs on the way to working disks ==

The first boot came up without disks. Fixing that turned up three separate problems.

'''1. Upstream DT pin conflict.''' <code>kirkwood-synology.dtsi</code> gives the SATA controller the <code>sata0</code>/<code>sata1</code> pin groups, which include MPP20-23 for the activity LEDs. But <code>kirkwood-ds212j.dts</code> also uses MPP20-23 as GPIO HDD LEDs, so pinctrl refused the SATA pins and <code>sata_mv</code> never probed:

<pre>kirkwood-pinctrl: pin PIN21 already requested by gpio-leds-hdd-21-2; cannot claim for f1080000.sata</pre>
The stock MPP registers show MPP20-23 set to GPIO, so the fix is to drop the SATA node's <code>pinctrl-0</code> on this board.

'''2. The drives are not powered.''' Once <code>sata_mv</code> probed, both links stayed down (<code>SStatus 0</code>). On the DS-212j, each drive's 5 V/12 V is switched by a GPIO that is off at reset. Under DSM, Synology's kernel switches it on. Driving the candidate lines from Linux with <code>gpioset</code> found them (<code>gpioset -c 0 -z 29=1</code>, and disk 1 appeared):

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! GPIO
! Function
|-
| 29
| HDD1 power (active high)
|-
| 31
| HDD2 power (active high)
|-
| 20/21
| HDD2/HDD1 green LED (active low)
|-
| 22/23
| HDD2/HDD1 amber LED (active low)
|-
| 32-34
| fan speed (3 bits)
|-
| 35
| fan tachometer/alarm input
|}

Synology's GPL kernel source for DSM 6.2 (<code>arch/arm/mach-feroceon-kw/synology-gpio.c</code>) confirms it. <code>KW_6281_212j_GPIO_init()</code> reuses the DS109 table (<code>hdd2_pm = 31</code>, fan 32-35, fail LEDs 22/23) and adds <code>hdd1_pm = 29</code>, and DSM waits 3 s between drives. The upstream <code>kirkwood-synology.dtsi</code> already has the matching <code>regulators-hdd-29</code> node; the DS-212j DT just never enabled it. The [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/tree/main/patches/linux DT patch] does that, and the U-Boot board code powers HDD1 at early init and HDD2 3 s later.

'''3. U-Boot gives up on the drives too early.''' Even when powered, U-Boot printed <code>Failed to identify SATA device</code>, but a manual <code>sata stop; sata init</code> at the prompt a minute later found both drives. Timing it showed that these Seagate ST32000644NS drives need '''~18 s''' after power-on before they answer ATA IDENTIFY, while <code>sata_mv</code> tried only twice, 10 ms apart. The second [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/tree/main/patches/u-boot U-Boot patch] keeps asking for up to 30 s once the link is up. Empty ports have no link, so they aren't delayed:

<pre>SATA0: waiting for drive to spin up done
SATA1: waiting for drive to spin up done</pre>
U-Boot also reads the factory MAC address from the <code>vendor</code> flash sector (6 bytes plus a checksum byte, the sum of the 6 bytes). Without it, U-Boot made up a random MAC on every boot.

<span id="step-9--flash-the-new-u-boot"></span>
== Step 9 – Flash the new U-Boot ==

From the RAM-loaded U-Boot, I loaded the image over the serial line with YMODEM (TFTP worked but stalled once, and serial is fast enough for 330 KB), checked its CRC against the file on the PC, and wrote it:

<pre>DS212j&gt; loady 0x1000000                 # PC: sb -k u-boot.kwb &lt;/dev/ttyUSB0 &gt;/dev/ttyUSB0
DS212j&gt; crc32 0x1000000 ${filesize}     # == crc32 of the file on the PC
DS212j&gt; sf probe
DS212j&gt; sf protect unlock 0 0x400000    # Synology leaves the block-protect bits set!
DS212j&gt; sf update 0x1000000 0 ${filesize}
DS212j&gt; sf read 0x2000000 0 ${filesize}
DS212j&gt; cmp.b 0x1000000 0x2000000 ${filesize}
Total of 329216 byte(s) were the same</pre>
Without <code>sf protect unlock</code>, <code>sf update</code> fails with <code>ERROR: flash area is locked</code> and writes nothing, which is harmless. Only the first 330 KB of flash change. The <code>vendor</code> sector, the old DSM kernel/ramdisk and the rest stay as they were, and <code>kwboot</code> plus the 4 MB backup can restore everything.

<span id="step-10--install-debian-on-a-raid1-mirror-of-the-two-disks"></span>
== Step 10 – Install Debian on a RAID1 mirror of the two disks ==

Booted from the USB stick, I partitioned both disks identically (MBR, since the disks are 2 TB):

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Partition
! Size
! Type
! Array
! Use
|-
| sdX1
| 16 GiB
| fd
| md0, '''metadata 0.90'''
| <code>/</code> (with <code>/boot</code>)
|-
| sdX2
| 2 GiB
| da
| md1, metadata 1.2
| swap
|-
| sdX3
| rest
| da
| md2, metadata 1.2 + bitmap
| <code>/srv</code>
|}

The 0.90 metadata for the root array is deliberate:

* The superblock sits at the '''end''' of the partition, so each member starts with a plain ext4 filesystem. U-Boot's bootstd reads <code>/boot/extlinux/extlinux.conf</code> straight from <code>sata 0:1</code> (or <code>sata 1:1</code> if the first disk dies) without knowing about RAID.
* The kernel '''autodetects''' 0.90 arrays on partitions of type <code>fd</code> (<code>CONFIG_MD_AUTODETECT</code>), so <code>root=/dev/md0</code> works '''without an initramfs'''.

The data and swap arrays use modern metadata, and mdadm assembles them in userspace from <code>/etc/mdadm/mdadm.conf</code>. [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/tools/install-to-raid.sh <code>tools/install-to-raid.sh</code>] creates the filesystems, copies the running system and writes <code>fstab</code>, <code>mdadm.conf</code> and <code>extlinux.conf</code>.

<pre>md: Autodetecting RAID arrays.
md: running: &lt;sdb1&gt;&lt;sda1&gt;
md/raid1:md0: active with 2 out of 2 mirrors
VFS: Mounted root (ext4 filesystem) on device 9:0.
Welcome to Debian GNU/Linux 13 (trixie)!</pre>
U-Boot scans SATA before USB, so the stick is no longer needed.

<span id="result"></span>
== Result ==

<pre>$ uname -r
7.2.10-ds212j
$ findmnt / /srv ; swapon
/dev/md0  /     ext4
/dev/md2  /srv  ext4   (1.8T)
/dev/md1  swap  2G
$ free -m
Mem:  239 total, 46 used</pre>
What works: both SATA disks in RAID1, gigabit Ethernet with the factory MAC, USB, the RTC, the fan (gpio-fan, 3800 rpm at the default speed), the hardware crypto engine (CESA), and the LEDs (disk, power, status) and buzzer behaving as under DSM (step 12). The serial console is at 115200 baud, and SSH works on the LAN.

<span id="step-11--the-kernel-as-a-debian-package"></span>
== Step 11 – The kernel as a Debian package ==

Copying a <code>zImage</code> by hand works, but <code>apt</code> doesn't know about it. The kernel's own <code>make bindeb-pkg</code> builds a proper <code>linux-image-7.2.10-ds212j</code> package, and Debian's '''u-boot-menu''' package regenerates <code>/boot/extlinux/extlinux.conf</code> from the kernel postinst/postrm hooks whenever a kernel is installed or removed. Each kernel gets an entry plus a &quot;rescue target&quot; entry.

[https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/tools/build-kernel-deb.sh <code>tools/build-kernel-deb.sh</code>] does everything: it downloads and verifies the tarball, applies the patches and config fragment, and builds the package.

<pre>sudo apt install debhelper            # bindeb-pkg's debian/rules calls dh_listpackages
tools/build-kernel-deb.sh 7.2.10 2    # -&gt; build/debs/linux-image-7.2.10-ds212j_7.2.10-2_armel.deb</pre>
Notes:

* The <code>linux-headers</code> package is skipped (build profile <code>pkg.linux-upstream.nokernelheaders</code>). It would need the armel OpenSSL headers on the build host and is only useful for DKMS.
* <code>bindeb-pkg</code> installs the DTBs '''flat''' in <code>/usr/lib/linux-image-&lt;version&gt;/</code>, without the <code>marvell/</code> subdirectory.

On the NAS, <code>/etc/default/u-boot</code>:

<pre>U_BOOT_PARAMETERS=&quot;console=ttyS0,115200 rootfstype=ext4 rootwait ro&quot;
U_BOOT_ROOT=&quot;root=/dev/md0&quot;
U_BOOT_TIMEOUT=&quot;30&quot;
U_BOOT_FDT=&quot;kirkwood-ds212j.dtb&quot;</pre>
Install the kernel '''before''' <code>u-boot-menu</code>. Otherwise its first run finds no <code>/boot/vmlinuz-*</code> and writes an empty menu, and the NAS won't boot:

<pre>sudo apt install ./linux-image-7.2.10-ds212j_7.2.10-2_armel.deb
sudo apt install u-boot-menu &amp;&amp; sudo u-boot-update</pre>
Upgrading a kernel is now an ordinary <code>apt install</code> of the new package:

<pre>U-Boot menu
1:  Debian GNU/Linux 7.2.10-ds212j
2:  Debian GNU/Linux 7.2.10-ds212j (rescue target)
Retrieving file: /boot/vmlinuz-7.2.10-ds212j
Retrieving file: /usr/lib/linux-image-7.2.10-ds212j/kirkwood-ds212j.dtb</pre>
<span id="step-12--leds-beep-and-fan-like-dsm"></span>
== Step 12 – LEDs, beep and fan, like DSM ==

<span id="disk-leds"></span>
=== Disk LEDs ===

Synology's GPL code (<code>synobios/mv6281/ds212j.c</code> and <code>synology-gpio.c</code>) shows how DSM drives them:

* '''Green''' is driven by the SATA controller itself. MPP20/21 are switched to their &quot;SATA activity&quot; function (<code>0x5</code>), and the SATA LED register is set to &quot;presence + activity&quot; (<code>SATAHC_LED_ACT_PRESENT</code>, bit 2 at <code>0x8002C</code>). The LED stays lit while a disk is present and blinks on I/O.
* '''Orange''' (fault) are plain GPIOs 22/23, active low.

Mainline <code>sata_mv</code> knows the register bit (<code>SOC_LED_CTRL_ACT_PRESENCE</code>) but never sets it. A [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/patches/linux/0002-ata-sata_mv-optional-presence-activity-LED-mode.patch small patch] adds a <code>marvell,led-act-presence</code> DT property for it. The DT routes MPP20/21 to the SATA controller, and only the orange LEDs remain <code>gpio-leds</code>.

The orange LEDs are lit from userspace by <code>ds212j-leds</code> (see below) when a disk is <code>faulty</code> in an md array or SMART reports it failing.

<span id="power-led-status-led-and-beep"></span>
=== Power LED, STATUS LED and beep ===

These are handled by the microcontroller on UART1 (<code>/dev/ttyS1</code>, 9600 8N1), which also handles power-off (<code>1</code>, sent by the mainline <code>synology,power-off</code> driver). Synology's GPL code only shows <code>4</code>/<code>6</code>. I tested every code below on this unit by sending it and watching the front panel. Commands can be sent back to back.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Code
! Effect
|-
| <code>4</code>
| power LED steady
|-
| <code>5</code>
| power LED blinking
|-
| <code>6</code>
| power LED off
|-
| <code>7</code>
| STATUS LED off
|-
| <code>8</code>
| STATUS LED green
|-
| <code>9</code>
| STATUS LED green, blinking
|-
| <code>:</code>
| STATUS LED orange
|-
| <code>;</code>
| STATUS LED orange, blinking
|-
| <code>3</code>
| the beep DSM gives when boot has finished
|-
| <code>2</code>
| a shorter beep
|}

<code>ds212j-microp</code> wraps these (<code>ds212j-microp power-led on</code>, <code>ds212j-microp status-led orange-blink</code>, <code>ds212j-microp beep</code>…). The behaviour follows the DS212j hardware guide:

* '''POWER:''' blinks while booting (the microcontroller does this by itself from power-on) and while shutting down. It goes steady once the system is up.
* '''Beep:''' one beep when boot has finished.
* '''STATUS:''' green when all md arrays are healthy. Blinking orange when one is degraded or crashed, or when no array exists.

A oneshot systemd unit sends &quot;power LED steady + beep + STATUS&quot; when the system is up, and &quot;power LED blinking&quot; when it's stopped at shutdown. <code>ds212j-leds</code> updates STATUS and the amber HDD LEDs from mdadm's monitor (<code>PROGRAM</code> in <code>mdadm.conf</code>, on every RAID event) and from a 10-minute timer. It only talks to the microcontroller when the STATUS state changes.

Both scripts and their units are packaged as [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/tree/main/pkg/ds212j-support <code>ds212j-support</code>], built with <code>dpkg-deb --root-owner-group -b pkg/ds212j-support</code>.

<span id="lan-led-dark-at-1000-mbs"></span>
=== LAN LED: dark at 1000 Mb/s ===

On a gigabit switch, the front LAN LED stayed off (at 100 Mb/s it worked). The LED is wired to '''LED[1] of the Marvell 88E1116R PHY''' (PHY ID <code>0x01410e40</code>, MDIO address 8). Synology's <code>synobios/mv6281/ds212j.c</code> (<code>SetPhyLed()</code>) programs only that LED, in page 3, register 16, bits 7:4: <code>0x1</code> for on (link/activity), <code>0x8</code> for off during hibernation.

Reading the register on the running system (standard MII ioctls on <code>eth0</code>) gave <code>0x101E</code>, the PHY's power-on default, which Linux doesn't touch for this PHY. LED[1] was already <code>0x1</code>, but LED[0] = <code>0xE</code> is one of Marvell's '''dual-LED modes''': LED[0] and LED[1] are driven as a pair, and at 1000 Mb/s the link is shown on the LED[0] pin instead. Taking LED[0] out of dual mode (<code>0x0</code>) makes the front LED work at every speed. I tested it on the hardware (register <code>0x1010</code>).

<code>ds212j-phy-led on|off|show</code> sets this, and a <code>/etc/network/if-up.d/</code> hook runs it whenever <code>eth0</code> comes up. During disk hibernation the LAN LED is forced off, like the other LEDs.

<span id="fan-an-interrupt-storm-in-the-upstream-dt"></span>
=== Fan: an interrupt storm in the upstream DT ===

After the first boot, <code>systemd-udevd</code> used ~25% CPU on an idle system. The upstream Synology dtsi gives <code>gpio-fan</code> GPIO 35 as <code>alarm-gpios</code>, with an edge interrupt. But on this board GPIO 35 is the fan '''tachometer''': it toggles all the time while the fan spins. Synology's own <code>GetFanStatus()</code> samples it 200 times and calls the fan &quot;running&quot; if it sees both levels. Every edge raised the &quot;alarm&quot; interrupt, and <code>gpio-fan</code> answered each one with a <code>sysfs_notify()</code> and a uevent:

<pre>$ grep fan /proc/interrupts
 35:     105970  f1010140.gpio   3 Edge   GPIO fan alarm
$ udevadm monitor --kernel     # 263 &quot;change&quot; events in 5 s</pre>
Deleting <code>alarm-gpios</code> for the DS-212j in the DT fixes it. Fan speed control is unaffected, and the uevents drop to zero.

<span id="step-13--fan-modes-like-dsm"></span>
== Step 13 – Fan modes like DSM ==

DSM offers four fan modes: '''Full-speed''', '''Cool''', '''Quiet''' and '''Low-power''' (&quot;the fan stops when the system is cold&quot;). The DS-212j has no board or CPU temperature sensor (<code>THERMAL_NO, CPUTMP_NO</code> in Synology's <code>mapping.h</code>), so DSM's <code>scemd</code> daemon drives the fan from the '''disk temperatures'''. Its tables come from the official firmware (<code>DSM_DS212j_25556.pat</code> → <code>hda1.tgz</code>, which is xz despite its name → <code>/usr/syno/etc.defaults/scemd.xml</code>):

<syntaxhighlight lang="xml"><fan_config period="20" threshold="6" type="DUAL_MODE_HIGH" hibernation_speed="STOP">       <!-- Cool -->
    <disk_temperature fan_speed="ULTRA_LOW"  action="NONE">0</disk_temperature>
    <disk_temperature fan_speed="VERY_LOW"   action="NONE">33</disk_temperature>
    <disk_temperature fan_speed="LOW"        action="NONE">41</disk_temperature>
    <disk_temperature fan_speed="VERY_HIGH"  action="NONE">47</disk_temperature>
    <disk_temperature fan_speed="ULTRA_HIGH" action="NONE">53</disk_temperature>
    <disk_temperature fan_speed="ULTRA_HIGH" action="SHUTDOWN">61</disk_temperature>
</fan_config>
<!-- DUAL_MODE_LOW (Quiet): 0 ULTRA_LOW, 38 VERY_LOW, 46 LOW, 52 VERY_HIGH, 58 ULTRA_HIGH, 61 SHUTDOWN
     DUAL_MODE_LOW_STOP (Low-power): 0 STOP, 33 ULTRA_LOW, then like Quiet --></syntaxhighlight>
The speed levels are the 3-bit value on GPIO 32-34 (<code>FanStatusMappingType1()</code>: STOP 0, ULTRA_LOW 1, VERY_LOW 2, LOW 4, VERY_HIGH 6, ULTRA_HIGH 7). The DT lists all eight values in that order (see the appendix), and <code>ds212j-fan</code> reproduces scemd '''exactly''':

* It polls every 20 s, using the hottest internal disk (<code>smartctl -n standby -A</code>, which never wakes a sleeping disk).
* It speeds up at once and slows down only after 6 polls in a row (<code>threshold=&quot;6&quot;</code>).
* The fan stops while the disks hibernate.
* It shuts the NAS down at 61 °C.

The mode is set in <code>/etc/default/ds212j-fan</code> (<code>MODE=full|cool|quiet|low-power</code>). Measured on this unit, level VERY_LOW (2) actually stops the fan. DSM does the same.

<span id="step-14--disk-hibernation-like-dsm"></span>
== Step 14 – Disk hibernation like DSM ==

The first version used only the drives' own standby timer (<code>hdparm -S</code>). The disks never slept. Two causes were found by measurement:

* '''The drives' timer never expires.''' <code>ds212j-fan</code> reads the disk temperatures with SMART every 20 s, like DSM's scemd. Those commands don't show up as I/O: three <code>smartctl</code> runs leave <code>/proc/diskstats</code> unchanged. But many drives count them as activity, which resets their internal idle timer.
* '''One last writer on the system disk.''' With the system idle, the only file modified on the root filesystem was <code>/var/lib/systemd/timesync/clock</code>, which <code>systemd-timesyncd</code> rewrites at every NTP sync (up to every ~34 minutes).

The fix does what DSM does, deciding in software when the disks are idle:

* '''Spin-down by <code>hd-idle</code>.''' It watches <code>/proc/diskstats</code> and sends <code>STANDBY IMMEDIATE</code> (<code>-c ata</code>) after <code>SPINDOWN_MINUTES</code> without real I/O (10 by default, set in <code>/etc/default/ds212j-disks</code>). It runs from its own unit, <code>ds212j-hd-idle.service</code>, limited to the two internal disks (<code>-i 0</code> for every other disk, so a USB disk is never spun down). Debian's own <code>hd-idle.service</code> is disabled: it ignores <code>START_HD_IDLE=false</code> and, with no options, would spin down every disk.
* '''timesyncd's state in RAM.''' A tmpfs is mounted on <code>/var/lib/systemd/timesync</code>. The board has a battery-backed RTC (S-35390A), so the clock file isn't needed across boots.
* '''Logs in RAM.''' journald uses <code>Storage=volatile</code> (16 MB), <code>rsyslog</code> is removed, and <code>ds212j-logsave</code> appends the journal to <code>/var/log/journal-saved/YYYY-MM.log</code> once a day and at every clean shutdown. A power cut loses at most a day of logs. The root filesystem is mounted <code>noatime</code>.
* '''The front panel looks like DSM's.''' The DS212j is <code>HIBER_LED_POWER_ONLY</code> in Synology's source. While both disks are in standby (<code>hdparm -C</code>, which doesn't wake them), only the power LED stays lit: STATUS goes off (<code>7</code>), the LAN LED is forced off, and the green HDD LEDs go off. The &quot;presence&quot; bit of the SATA LED register (<code>0xF108002C</code> bit 2) is cleared through <code>/dev/mem</code>, so the LEDs only blink on activity. The fan stops too. Everything returns when a disk wakes up.

To check whether the disks are really asleep: <code>sudo hdparm -C /dev/sda /dev/sdb</code>.

To find what wakes them, <code>CONFIG_TASK_IO_ACCOUNTING</code> is enabled in the kernel config, so <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/io</code> and <code>iotop</code> work. Without it, the files modified recently on the root filesystem (<code>find / -xdev -mmin -3</code>) and the counters in <code>/proc/diskstats</code> are the only clues.

<span id="step-15--copyparty-file-server"></span>
== Step 15 – copyparty file server ==

[https://github.com/9001/copyparty copyparty] suits this box much better than Nextcloud. Nextcloud needs PHP, a database and 512 MB+ of RAM, and 32-bit support is being deprecated. copyparty is a single Python file that gives a web UI with resumable uploads, WebDAV, search, and thumbnails (<code>python3-pil</code>, <code>ffmpeg</code>).

* The official single-file release, <code>copyparty-sfx.py</code>, is checked against the SHA-256 published on GitHub and installed in <code>/usr/local/bin</code>.
* It runs as a dedicated <code>copyparty</code> user under a hardened unit ([https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/config/copyparty/copyparty.service <code>config/copyparty/copyparty.service</code>]): <code>ProtectSystem=strict</code>, with only <code>/srv/files</code> and <code>/var/lib/copyparty</code> writable.
* The config is [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/config/copyparty/copyparty.conf.template <code>/etc/copyparty/copyparty.conf</code>]: one account with full rights on <code>/srv/files</code> (RAID1), no anonymous access, and the index and thumbnails kept in <code>/var/lib/copyparty</code>.

<pre>sudo apt install --no-install-recommends python3-pil ffmpeg
sudo useradd --system --home-dir /var/lib/copyparty --create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin copyparty
sudo install -m 755 copyparty-sfx.py /usr/local/bin/
sudo install -d -o copyparty -g copyparty /srv/files
sudo systemctl enable --now copyparty        # -&gt; http://ds212j:3923/</pre>
<span id="step-16--the-network-dies-under-memory-pressure"></span>
== Step 16 – The network dies under memory pressure ==

While copying 1.5 TB onto the NAS (a local copy from a USB disk through <code>ntfs-3g</code>, plus an SMB mount of another NAS, with copyparty indexing the new files), the DS-212j dropped off the network: no ping, no ARP reply. The disk LEDs kept blinking. The system was still running and the copy went on, but '''nothing left the box'''. Unplugging and replugging the cable brought it back, and the kernel log showed why:

<pre>mv643xx_eth_port mv643xx_eth_port.0 eth0: failed to linearize skb with tiny unaligned fragment
...                                              (18124 times in 4 minutes)
mv643xx_eth_port mv643xx_eth_port.0 eth0: link down
mv643xx_eth_port mv643xx_eth_port.0 eth0: link up, 1000 Mb/s, full duplex, flow control disabled</pre>
The Kirkwood Ethernet controller can't send a packet whose fragments are too small or unaligned. For those, <code>mv643xx_eth</code> calls <code>skb_linearize()</code>, which needs a new contiguous buffer. With 256 MB of RAM almost full (<code>kswapd</code> busy, 12 MB free), that allocation fails and the driver drops the packet. Every outgoing packet ends up dropped. The link reset only helped for a moment: the errors started again right after the cable was back, at 50–150 per second.

The fix is not to hand the driver fragmented packets at all:

* '''No scatter-gather and no TSO on <code>eth0</code>''': <code>ethtool -K eth0 sg off tso off</code>, applied by a udev rule every time <code>eth0</code> appears. The kernel then builds each packet in one linear buffer itself. The errors stopped at the exact second this was applied, and the copy went on at the same speed (the CPU is the bottleneck anyway, not the NIC).
* '''A larger free-memory reserve''': <code>vm.min_free_kbytes = 8192</code> (the kernel picked 1979 for 256 MB), so that atomic allocations in the network path find memory.

Both are in <code>ds212j-support</code> 1.8 (<code>/usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-ds212j-eth0-no-sg.rules</code>, <code>/usr/lib/sysctl.d/60-ds212j-mem.conf</code>). Also, keep copyparty from indexing a large volume while it is being filled (volume flag <code>d2d</code>), because its indexer added to the memory pressure.

