<span id="appendix--the-device-tree-changes-explained"></span>
== Appendix – The device-tree changes explained ==

The DS-212j [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/patches/linux/0001-ARM-dts-kirkwood-ds212j-fix-RAM-SATA-HDD-power-and-LEDs.patch DT patch] makes six changes, all to <code>arch/arm/boot/dts/marvell/kirkwood-ds212j.dts</code>. Each one is a place where the upstream description of the board didn't match the real hardware. This appendix explains what changed, and above all '''how I found out what to change'''. Every change rests on at least two independent sources.

<span id="background-how-the-ds-212j-device-tree-is-built"></span>
=== Background: how the DS-212j device tree is built ===

The DTB tells the generic Kirkwood kernel what is on this particular board. It is built from four files:

<pre>kirkwood.dtsi            → the SoC itself (UARTs, SATA, USB, GPIO controllers…)
kirkwood-6281.dtsi       → the 88F6281 variant (pin groups such as &quot;sata0&quot;, &quot;sata1&quot;)
kirkwood-synology.dtsi   → blocks shared by 16 Synology models, mostly
                           status = &quot;disabled&quot;, for each model to switch on
kirkwood-ds212j.dts      → the DS-212j: switches on the blocks that apply</pre>
The board file is short, and that's where the bugs were. It switched on the wrong shared blocks, or didn't switch on the right ones. The shared <code>.dtsi</code> files are left untouched, and every fix is an override in <code>kirkwood-ds212j.dts</code>, so no other Synology model is affected.

<span id="where-the-information-came-from"></span>
=== Where the information came from ===

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Source
! What it gave
|-
| '''The stock U-Boot''', over the serial console
| the boot banner, the DDR register list (step 2), and the pin-mux (MPP) and GPIO register values
|-
| '''Synology's GPL source''' for the DSM 6.2 kernel (<code>linux-2.6.32</code>, model <code>6281</code>) and its <code>synobios</code> module, from Synology's download site (''ToolChain → Synology NAS GPL Source'')
| the GPIO map and LED handling Synology itself uses on the DS212j
|-
| '''Experiments on the running NAS'''
| kernel error messages, toggling GPIOs with <code>gpioset</code>, counting interrupts and udev events
|-
| '''Watching the front panel'''
| which LED or beep reacts to which command
|}

The Synology files that matter are <code>arch/arm/mach-feroceon-kw/synology-gpio.c</code> (the per-model GPIO tables), <code>arch/arm/mach-kirkwood/include/mach/synology.h</code>, and <code>synobios/mv6281/ds212j.c</code> (DS212j-specific LED and fan code).

<span id="1-ram-128-mb--256-mb"></span>
=== 1. RAM: 128 MB → 256 MB ===

<syntaxhighlight lang="diff">-       reg = <0x00000000 0x8000000>;
+       reg = <0x00000000 0x10000000>;  /* 256 MB: 2 x 128 MB chip selects */</syntaxhighlight>
'''Evidence:'''

<ul>
<li>The stock U-Boot banner shows two 128 MB chip selects:
<pre>DRAM CS[0] base 0x00000000   size 128MB
DRAM CS[1] base 0x08000000   size 128MB
DRAM Total size 256MB  16bit width</pre></li>
<li>The DDR init list in the stock bootloader header enables both: <code>0xFFD01504 = 0x07FFFFF1</code> (CS0, 128 MB, enabled) and <code>0xFFD0150C = 0x07FFFFF5</code> (CS1, 128 MB, enabled).</li>
<li>After the change Linux reports <code>node 0: [mem 0x00000000-0x0fffffff]</code>, and <code>free</code> shows 239 MB usable.</li></ul>

Mainline U-Boot fixes up the memory node at boot anyway, but the DT should be right for any bootloader.

<span id="2-disk-power-enable-regulators-hdd-29"></span>
=== 2. Disk power: enable <code>regulators-hdd-29</code> ===

<syntaxhighlight lang="diff">+   /* HDD1/HDD2 power switches: GPIO 29 and 31, both off at reset */
+   regulators-hdd-29 {
+       status = "okay";
+   };</syntaxhighlight>
'''Symptom:''' once SATA was probing (see #3), both ports stayed at <code>ata1: SATA link down (SStatus 0 SControl F300)</code>. No disk was even electrically present, yet DSM had seen both.

'''How the pins were found:''' tangrs had already found GPIO 29/31 in 2021, from Synology's source and by toggling them through <code>/sys/class/gpio</code> until the drives spun up. That [https://gist.github.com/tangrs/c3e6bd13b849d5a5bcf9d338550427fe gist] enables the same <code>regulators-hdd-29</code> block. I re-checked it independently on this unit:

<ol style="list-style-type: decimal;">
<li><code>kirkwood-synology.dtsi</code> lists the HDD-power GPIOs used by the various models: 29, 30, 31, 34, 44 and 45.</li>
<li>On the running NAS, <code>gpioget</code> showed all of them as inputs reading 0, so nothing was powering the disks.</li>
<li>I drove them high one at a time and rescanned SATA:
<pre># gpioset -c 0 -z 29=1; sleep 10; echo &quot;- - -&quot; &gt; /sys/class/scsi_host/host0/scan
ata1: SATA link up 3.0 Gbps (SStatus 123 SControl F300)
ata1.00: ATA-8: ST32000644NS, SN11, max UDMA/133</pre>
GPIO 29 powers disk 1, and GPIO 31 does the same for disk 2.</li>
<li>Synology's source confirms it (<code>synology-gpio.c</code>). The DS212j reuses the DS109 table, which has <code>.hdd2_pm = 31</code>, and adds HDD1:
<syntaxhighlight lang="c">KW_6281_212j_GPIO_init(SYNO_KW_GENERIC_GPIO *global_gpio)
{
 KW_6281_109_GPIO_init(global_gpio);
 global_gpio->hdd_pm.hdd1_pm = 29;
}</syntaxhighlight>
<code>libata</code> switches a disk on with <code>SYNO_CTRL_HDD_POWERON(port, 1)</code>, so the line is '''active high''', and DSM waits 3 s between the two disks.</li></ol>

'''The fix:''' <code>kirkwood-synology.dtsi</code> already describes exactly this, in a disabled block called <code>regulators-hdd-29</code>: two always-on fixed regulators named <code>hdd1power</code> (GPIO 29) and <code>hdd2power</code> (GPIO 31), active high, each with a 5 s start-up delay. The DS-212j file simply never enabled it. The new U-Boot does the same in its board code (HDD1 at early init, HDD2 3 s later), so the disks are already spinning when U-Boot scans SATA and when Linux starts.

<span id="3-sata-pin-conflict-route-only-mpp2021-to-the-sata-controller"></span>
=== 3. SATA pin conflict: route only MPP20/21 to the SATA controller ===

<syntaxhighlight lang="diff">+&pinctrl {
+   pmx_sata0_act_21: pmx-sata0-act-21 {
+       marvell,pins = "mpp21";
+       marvell,function = "sata0";
+   };
+
+   pmx_sata1_act_20: pmx-sata1-act-20 {
+       marvell,pins = "mpp20";
+       marvell,function = "sata1";
+   };
+};
+
+&sata {
+   pinctrl-0 = <&pmx_sata0_act_21 &pmx_sata1_act_20>;
+   pinctrl-names = "default";
+   marvell,led-act-presence;   /* see #5 */
+};</syntaxhighlight>
'''Symptom:''' on the very first boot, the SATA driver didn't even load:

<pre>kirkwood-pinctrl f1010000.pin-controller: pin PIN21 already requested by gpio-leds-hdd-21-2; cannot claim for f1080000.sata
sata_mv f1080000.sata: error -EINVAL: Error applying setting, reverse things back</pre>
'''Cause:''' the SoC's pins are multi-purpose (&quot;MPP&quot;), and two blocks claimed the same ones:

* <code>kirkwood-synology.dtsi</code> gives the SATA controller the <code>sata0</code> and <code>sata1</code> pin groups, which <code>kirkwood-6281.dtsi</code> defines as MPP 5/21/23 and MPP 4/20/22 (the activity/presence LED outputs);
* <code>kirkwood-ds212j.dts</code> enabled <code>gpio-leds-hdd-21-2</code>, which uses MPP 20-23 as plain GPIOs.

The kernel won't give a pin to two drivers, so <code>sata_mv</code> gave up.

'''What each pin should be:''' three sources agree.

<ul>
<li>'''The stock pin-mux registers''', read from the stock U-Boot prompt:
<pre>Marvell&gt;&gt; md.l f1010000 7
f1010000: 01002222 03303311 00550000 00000000</pre>
The third word covers MPP16-23, one nibble per pin starting from MPP16. <code>00550000</code> means MPP 20-23 are function 0 (GPIO) at boot, and the first word shows MPP 4/5 are GPIO as well.</li>
<li>'''Synology's DS212j LED code''' (<code>synobios/mv6281/ds212j.c</code>, <code>SetHDDActLed()</code>): to light the disk LEDs, DSM switches MPP 20/21 to function <code>0x5</code>:
<syntaxhighlight lang="c">case SYNO_LED_ON:
  SYNOMppCtrlRegWrite(20, 0x5);
  SYNOMppCtrlRegWrite(21, 0x5);</syntaxhighlight></li>
<li>'''The kernel's pin table''' (<code>drivers/pinctrl/mvebu/pinctrl-kirkwood.c</code>) names that function:
<syntaxhighlight lang="c">MPP_VAR_FUNCTION(0x5, "sata1", "act", V(0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0)),   /* MPP20 */
MPP_VAR_FUNCTION(0x5, "sata0", "act", V(0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0)),   /* MPP21 */</syntaxhighlight>
It's valid on the 88F6281.</li></ul>

So the DT gains two pin-mux nodes (MPP21 → <code>sata0</code> act = HDD1 green, MPP20 → <code>sata1</code> act = HDD2 green), and the SATA node claims only those two pins instead of the generic six. MPP 22/23 stay GPIOs for the orange LEDs, and MPP 4/5 are left alone.

<span id="4-disk-leds-replace-gpio-leds-hdd-21-2-with-gpio-leds-hdd-fault"></span>
=== 4. Disk LEDs: replace <code>gpio-leds-hdd-21-2</code> with <code>gpio-leds-hdd-fault</code> ===

<syntaxhighlight lang="diff">-   gpio-leds-hdd-21-2 {
-       status = "okay";
-   };
+   gpio-leds-hdd-fault {
+       compatible = "gpio-leds";
+       pinctrl-0 = <&pmx_hddled_23 &pmx_hddled_22>;
+       pinctrl-names = "default";
+
+       led-hdd1-amber {
+           label = "synology:amber:hdd1";
+           gpios = <&gpio0 23 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+           default-state = "off";
+       };
+
+       led-hdd2-amber {
+           label = "synology:amber:hdd2";
+           gpios = <&gpio0 22 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+           default-state = "off";
+       };
+   };</syntaxhighlight>
'''Why:''' with the upstream node, all four disk LEDs were GPIO LEDs that nothing ever lit, so the front panel stayed dark. DSM uses two different mechanisms:

<ul>
<li>'''Green''' is hardware, driven by the SATA controller through MPP20/21 (#3 and #5).</li>
<li>'''Orange''' is software. <code>synology-gpio.c</code> has <code>.hdd1_fail_led = 23, .hdd2_fail_led = 22</code>, and the LED function writes <code>fail_led = 0</code> for orange and <code>1</code> for off/green, so the LEDs are '''active low''':
<syntaxhighlight lang="c">} else if ( DISK_LED_ORANGE_SOLID == status ||
  DISK_LED_ORANGE_BLINK == status ) {
  fail_led = 0;</syntaxhighlight></li></ul>

The new node therefore keeps only the two orange LEDs, under the same names as before (<code>synology:amber:hdd1/2</code> in <code>/sys/class/leds/</code>). The <code>ds212j-leds</code> script (step 12) lights them when a disk is faulty in the RAID or fails SMART.

<span id="5-marvellled-act-presence-green--present-blinking--activity"></span>
=== 5. <code>marvell,led-act-presence</code>: green = present, blinking = activity ===

Routing MPP20/21 to the SATA controller isn't quite enough. By default the controller only lights its &quot;act&quot; outputs '''during I/O'''. DSM's green LEDs are '''on while a disk is present''' and blink on activity.

<ul>
<li>'''DSM:''' the same Synology LED function begins with
<syntaxhighlight lang="c">MV_REG_WRITE(SATAHC_LED_CONFIG_REG, SATAHC_LED_ACT_PRESENT);</syntaxhighlight>
and <code>mach-kirkwood/include/mach/synology.h</code> defines <code>SATAHC_LED_CONFIG_REG = SATA base | 0x2C</code> and <code>SATAHC_LED_ACT_PRESENT = 0x4</code>.</li>
<li>'''Mainline Linux:''' <code>drivers/ata/sata_mv.c</code> already knows that register and bit, <code>SOC_LED_CTRL = 0x2c</code> and <code>SOC_LED_CTRL_ACT_PRESENCE = (1 &lt;&lt; 2)</code> (&quot;Multiplex dev presence with dev activity LED&quot;), but nothing ever sets it.</li></ul>

This is the only change that also needs a driver patch, [https://github.com/lepirlouit/debian-synology-ds212j/blob/main/patches/linux/0002-ata-sata_mv-optional-presence-activity-LED-mode.patch <code>0002-ata-sata_mv-optional-presence-activity-LED-mode.patch</code>]. It adds an optional <code>marvell,led-act-presence</code> DT property, and when the property is present <code>sata_mv</code> sets the bit at initialisation. Using a property means only boards that ask for it change behaviour.

<span id="6-fan-delete-alarm-gpios"></span>
=== 6. Fan: delete <code>alarm-gpios</code> ===

<syntaxhighlight lang="diff">    gpio-fan-100-32-35 {
        status = "okay";
+       /delete-property/ alarm-gpios;
    };</syntaxhighlight>
'''Symptom:''' <code>systemd-udevd</code> used about 25% CPU on an idle system. tangrs describes exactly this (19% idle CPU) in the post, and confirmed the tachometer by stopping the fan by hand and watching the pin stop toggling.

'''Diagnosis:'''

<ul>
<li><code>gpio-fan-100-32-35</code> in <code>kirkwood-synology.dtsi</code> uses GPIO 32-34 as the 3-bit speed selector and GPIO 35 as <code>alarm-gpios</code>. For an alarm line, the <code>gpio-fan</code> driver requests an edge interrupt and, on each one, sends a <code>sysfs_notify()</code> and a uevent so userspace can react to a fan failure.</li>
<li>But Synology's <code>ds212j.c</code> shows GPIO 35 is the fan's '''tachometer'''. <code>GetFanStatus()</code> samples it up to 200 times, 300 µs apart, and reports the fan as running only if it sees '''both''' levels. While the fan spins, it's a square wave.</li>
<li>Measured on the NAS:
<pre>$ grep -i fan /proc/interrupts
 35:     105970  f1010140.gpio   3 Edge      GPIO fan alarm
$ sudo timeout 5 udevadm monitor --kernel | grep -c change
263</pre>
Every tach edge was handled as a &quot;fan alarm&quot;, which meant a uevent and a wake-up of <code>systemd-udevd</code>, about 50 times a second.</li></ul>

'''The fix:''' <code>/delete-property/</code> removes that single property for the DS-212j. Fan speed control keeps working (gpio-fan, <code>fan1_input</code> in hwmon), the interrupt disappears, and the uevents drop to 0. GPIO 35 is now free for a userspace fan-failure check that counts its edges, as DSM does.

tangrs fixed it differently: the gist enables the shared <code>gpio-fan-150-32-35</code> block instead of <code>gpio-fan-100-32-35</code>. That block has no <code>alarm-gpios</code>, but it also has a '''different speed table''':

<pre>gpio-fan-100-32-35:  0→0  2500→1  3100→2  3800→3  4600→4  4800→5  4900→6  5000→7
gpio-fan-150-32-35:  0→0  2200→1  2500→2  3000→4  3300→3  3700→5  3800→6  4200→7</pre>
The <code>-150</code> ordering (value 4 below value 3) matches DSM's own <code>FanStatusMappingType1()</code> in <code>synobios/mapping.c</code>, which maps VERY_LOW → 2, MIDDLE → 3, LOW → 4, HIGH → 5. To decide which table is right, I '''measured''' the fan, now that GPIO 35 is free. For each value I waited 15 s, then counted tach pulses for 10 s (<code>gpiomon -c 1 -e rising 3</code>), assuming 2 pulses per revolution:

